<h2>What the Act actually built</h2>
<p>The Mediation Act, 2023 did four structural things. It defined and regulated mediation — including institutional mediation, online mediation and community mediation — with a Mediation Council of India to register mediators and recognise mediation service providers and institutes. It fixed the process: mediation is to be completed within a stated period (one hundred and twenty days from first appearance, extendable by consent up to a further sixty), confidentiality is statutory, and the mediator cannot be compelled to disclose what happened inside the room. It made the outcome enforceable: the mediated settlement agreement executes like a decree. And it protected the voluntariness of the process: a party may withdraw after the initial sittings, so the compulsion — where it exists under other statutes — is to attempt, not to agree.</p>

<h2>The Section 12A junction</h2>
<p>Commercial litigants meet mediation mainly through Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act: a suit that does not contemplate urgent interim relief may not be instituted without exhausting pre-institution mediation. The mandatory character of that requirement means a plaint filed in its breach is returned or rejected, so the mediation stage is unavoidable in ordinary recovery and contract claims. The Mediation Act upgrades what that stage can produce. Before it, a Section 12A settlement had the status conferred by the Commercial Courts regime; now, settlement agreements emerging from statutory mediation carry decree-like enforceability with a narrow challenge window. For a creditor, that changes the calculus: a defendant\'s offer of staged payments recorded as a mediated settlement agreement is no longer a gentleman\'s promise — default moves the matter straight to execution.</p>

<h2>Designing the clause and the strategy</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Tiered clauses</strong><p>Contract clauses can now sensibly provide mediation under the Act before arbitration or litigation, with named institutions — the tier is enforceable in substance because its product is enforceable.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Urgent relief carve-out</strong><p>Where interim protection is genuinely needed — assets dissipating, goods perishing — the Section 12A exception and the courts remain available; mediation does not trap the claimant.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Confidentiality</strong><p>Positions taken, admissions made and documents produced for mediation are shielded; the litigation record starts clean if the process fails.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Timeline discipline</strong><p>The statutory outer limits prevent mediation from becoming the delay tactic it once was; a party stalling simply exhausts the stage and the suit proceeds.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Execution and the narrow challenge</h2>
<p>Enforcement follows the CPC\'s execution machinery: the settlement holder applies for execution as a decree-holder would, with attachment, garnishee and the other modes available. The challenge grounds — fraud, corruption, impersonation, unfitness for mediation — are closer to the grounds for impeaching a judgment than a contract, and they are time-boxed. This is the Act\'s central trade: parties get finality and speed, and surrender the general contract-law defences they would have had against a mere settlement deed. Counsel drafting a mediated settlement agreement should treat it with decree-level care — precise payment schedules, default consequences, interest on default, and authentication by the mediator in the statutory form — because the document will be read by an executing court, not renegotiated.</p>

<h2>The realistic assessment</h2>
<p>Institutional capacity is still building, and parties in asymmetric disputes — a small supplier against a large buyer — should be alert to mediation used as a pressure valve rather than a resolution forum. But for the broad middle of commercial disputes, where the real question is how much and when rather than whether, the Act has quietly supplied what the system lacked: a disposal route that is faster than trial, cheaper than arbitration, and ends in a document the sheriff will enforce.</p>

<div class="note">
